● map()
It takes every item from an array and returns something for each item in the UI.
Array values → map() → JSX elements → React renders them → UI

● Keys
When React renders a collection, every item should have a unique key.
It is uniquely identifies each list item for React.

Example :-
{employees.map((employee) => (
  <div key={employee.id}>
    <h3>{employee.name}</h3>
    <p>{employee.role}</p>
  </div>
))}

=========================================
Why do we need key & key should be unique
=========================================

Imagine React has :-
Arun
Ravi
Kiran

with IDs :-
1
2
3

Later the data changes :-
Arun
Kiran

Ravi was removed.

React needs a way to identify :-
Which item was removed ?
Which item stayed ?
Which item changed ?

The key helps React identify each list item.

Think of it like an employee ID.
Arun  → ID 1
Ravi  → ID 2
Kiran → ID 3

The name might change :-
Arun → Arun Kumar

but the employee ID remains :-
ID 1

So React can identify the same item.

====================================================
Don't use Array Index as Key when you have a real ID
====================================================

Example :-
{employees.map((employee, index) => (
  <div key={index}>
    {employee.name}
  </div>
))}

This works technically, but it is generally better to use a stable unique ID when your data has one.

Prefer :-
key={employee.id}

instead of :-
key={index}

Suppose :-
Index 0 → Arun
Index 1 → Ravi
Index 2 → Kiran

Remove Arun :-
Index 0 → Ravi
Index 1 → Kiran

The indexes changed.

Array index starts from 0 in JavaScript.

But IDs remain stable :-
Ravi  → ID 2
Kiran → ID 3

So React can track the items more reliably.

========================
key is not a normal prop
========================

Consider :-
<Employee key={employee.id} employee={employee} />

Inside Employee :-
function Employee(props) {
  console.log(props.key);
}

You should not expect key to be available as a normal prop.

If the component needs the ID, pass it separately :-
<Employee
  key={employee.id}
  id={employee.id}
  employee={employee}
/>

Then :-
function Employee({ id, employee }) {
  console.log(id);
}

So :-
key={employee.id}

is mainly for React's internal list tracking.

● Rendering Collections
A collection is simply multiple items.
It displaying multiple items from an array in the UI.

Example1 ➔ Array of Strings
const users = ["Arun", "Ravi", "Kiran"];

Example2 ➔ Array of Numbers
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];

Example3 ➔ Array of Objects ⭐ Very common in real projects
const products = [
  { id: 101, name: "Laptop", price: 50000 },
  { id: 102, name: "Mobile", price: 20000 },
  { id: 103, name: "Headphones", price: 3000 }
];

Then in return block :-
{products.map((product) => (
  <div key={product.id}>
    <h3>{product.name}</h3>
    <p>{product.price}</p>
  </div>
))}

This is a collection of products.
